From addc57f73f8bd78258ba47a50cb44e5ad4deeca8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Majid Arasskhani <M.Arasskhani@gosilkroad.net>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 08:08:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 22/23] feat(logs): show the failure reason and the request
 type in API Logs
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The page fetched `error_message` and rendered "Fail", and nothing said
whether a row was a chat turn, a video job or an embedding call — so a
failed request could only be investigated by reading the server's own logs.

The list rows now carry the operation type (read from the usage operation)
and the failure detail; the table gains a Type column and a status cell that
names the reason with the full text on hover. Two filters come with it —
request type and failure reason — both offered from the values actually
present, next to the existing ones.

The detail modal opens with a Failure block for a failed request (reason,
HTTP status, provider job id, and the correlation id to grep the server log
with) and each upstream attempt now shows its connection, quantity and
timing alongside the costs it already had.

Export follows the page: Type, Error Code, HTTP Status, Error, Correlation
Id and Provider Job Id in the list CSV, plus the per-attempt connection,
window, quantity and error in the single-request CSV.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UTnpHajSmkk2JMLfuQoV7o
